WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.120
OK.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.520
I guess, let's start it.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.850
So welcome back.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.710
So today's lecture is going
to be on asymmetric numeral

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.520
systems.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.400
So let's just go over
the quiz from last time.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.730
So last time, we learnt
about Arithmetic coding.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.460
And the quiz question was
again around a Bernoulli

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.810
random variable, which we have
been now seeing quite a lot,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.470
where probability of A is p
and probability of B is just 1

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.369
minus p.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:38.010
And you were asked to consider
two symbols AB and BA, both

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:42.540
of them to be encoded
using Arithmetic coding.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.710
And the first part was, do
the codewords for AB and BA

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.985
have the same code length?

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:51.690
Yeah.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.160
OK, I see a couple of nodding
heads and that's indeed correct.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.710
I think most of
you got this right.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:05.349
In our case, both probability
of AB is going to be p times 1

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:09.320
minus p, which is same
as probability of BA.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.420
This is if you want to write it.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.120
And as Shubham discussed
in last lecture,

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.450
the codeword length just depends
on the interval length, which

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.890
depends on the probability.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.260
So in this case, both
of them will have

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.660
the exact same codeword length.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.300
And then the follow-up
question was like,

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.290
does our Arithmetic coder
output the same codeword?

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.125
Yeah?

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:38.440
The 1.1, the minor point is the
upper bound of the code length

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.540
depends on the project.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.510
In practice, for a
real Arithmetic coder,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.338
the code lengths might be
different by up to two-bits.

00:01:47.338 --> 00:01:48.880
So that's why in
the question I think

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.488
we added a statement
that you have

00:01:50.488 --> 00:01:52.780
to assume an idealized version
of the Arithmetic coder.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.590
Otherwise, it's not
actually strictly true.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.640
Yeah.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.240
Thank you.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.070
Yeah, good point.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.120
Is that clear?

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.180
So what Shubham just said
is like, in the actual--

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.764
if you remember like the
codeword length actually was--

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.300
so this was like the bound
for our codeword length.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.010
And so you'd actually need
an idealized compressor,

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:29.865
which can do this.

00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:31.740
And in this particular
case, you can work out

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.770
the exact probabilities.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.750
OK.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.090
Yeah, continuing the
second part was like,

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.950
do they output
the same codeword.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.620
And the answer there
is obviously false.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.990
They are both-- if we look at
the zero-one line interval,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:54.520
AB and BA would go for
the different regions.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.830
Sorry, we just expanded out.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:04.580
So your BA would
be in this region,

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:07.550
whereas your AB
would be expanded out

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.070
in this particular region.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.260
And so they are just
different codewords.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.750
Quite straightforward.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.770
Any doubts on that one?

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:19.270
No.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:19.770
OK.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.000
So continuing on
the second part,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.890
so then we basically gave you
some probability distribution

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.190
over symbols and gave
the probabilities.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:33.070
And then given an
input bitstream

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:37.320
and an input of length 3, we
ask what's the decoded sequence.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.760
So it's a very standard
Arithmetic decoding.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.910
You guys just had to go
back and redo what we learnt

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:43.940
in the class.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.110
So in this case, I've already
converted this bitstream

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.940
corresponds to 0.609375.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.480
And so all you need
to do is repeat--

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.980
sorry, repeat the
decoding procedure.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:07.810
So A contains 0.5
mass, B contains 0.25,

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.650
and similarly, C contains
0.25, and your number

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.180
is somewhere here.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.770
So the first symbol
you will output

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:20.680
is B. And then you zoom
in and you repeat, again,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.720
divided into--

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.360
for three intervals
and find the point.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.370
And if you keep doing
that, you basically

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.510
get the BAC as your
output codeword.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:36.330
OK.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.330
Any questions on Arithmetic
coding before we get

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.170
started with ANS?

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.070
Cool.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.970
Before we start, I just
want to give a shout out.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.190
Most of these slides are
similar from the last year.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:53.670
And so I just wanted
to give him credit.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.210
OK.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.120
So last class, we learned
about Arithmetic coding.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.350
And there, we learned about
that given any distribution p,

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.380
your Arithmetic coder
achieves optimal compression.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.270
And one really
important point there

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.520
was what Arithmetic
coding allows

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.430
is to separate the model
and entropy coding part.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.390
So you can come up with a best
model for whatever distributed--

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.420
whatever system you have.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.330
You can come up with the
probability for those blocks.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.340
And then the entropy
coding part takes

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.830
care of ensuring that
the lengths basically

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:31.660
have this bound, which we
just looked at in the class.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:35.970
So basically, it allows you
to come up with a good model

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.950
separately, and then
just use the code

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.870
as is, which was not something
we were able to do in Huffman

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.300
or Shannon or any of
the previous codes,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.280
which we looked at.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.590
This also meant that
it can work very well

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.330
with changing distribution p.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.660
In fact, you can come up with
adaptive algorithms, which

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.390
adapt to your changing
data distribution using

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.320
Arithmetic coding.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.080
And that's going to be a
topic, which we'll learn in--

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.330
which we'll get to, I think,
in two lectures from now.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.630
We'll show some
concrete examples there.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:11.050
Yeah, we had-- there are
various variants of arithmetic

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.060
coding in practice, which
you will find mostly

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:16.990
depends on how we break down
the ties, how we basically

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.395
have an efficient implementation
of the encoder and decoder.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.370
And basically, Arithmetic
coders are used everywhere.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.560
So again, we'll see
more and more examples

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.750
of this in future lectures.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.550
But then what's the issue?

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:37.780
So these were discovered
some time back in 1970s.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.850
So are we done?

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:42.070
Is lossless compression
a solved problem?

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:43.210
Interestingly, no.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:46.120
So again, I think Shubham talked
about it in the last lecture,

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.430
but if you look at
the compression,

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:51.250
so this is bits per
symbol, lower is better,

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.860
Arithmetic coding does much
better than Huffman coding.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.010
Again, it was because you can
imagine the whole block length--

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.320
whole data is part
of the block length.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:03.310
But interestingly, when you
compare the speed of Arithmetic

00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:05.950
and Huffman, both
the encode speed

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.810
is lower, as well
as the decode speed

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.450
is lower, and
actually quite lower.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.670
And dependent on
the application,

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.500
you might actually
want to trade off

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.420
the compression you
can get with the speed

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.910
because you might just have
to deal with a lot of data

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.070
very fast.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:24.340
And these numbers are from
Charles Bloom's blog, which

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.310
is very, very interesting.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:31.090
And so today, we'll learn
about rANS and tANS.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.550
And if you look at the
compression for rANS and tANS,

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.150
they have basically the same
similar compression performance,

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.800
but they have much higher at
least decompression speed.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.110
We'll come to the encode speed.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.910
But if you look at the
decompression speed,

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:50.000
they have much higher magnitude.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.240
So if you were to compare
something like tANS and Huffman,

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.890
they basically have very
similar decode speed.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:00.530
tANS has much higher encode
speed than Arithmetic,

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:03.140
while it performs
almost similarly

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.380
though with some slight
overhead compared to Arithmetic

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.180
in this particular example.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.440
And this is the reason why we
want to even study these codes.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.850
These are really efficient.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.990
And basically-- so
these were discovered,

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.060
I think, around 2014.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.120
And if you look at
any compressors today,

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:25.740
they basically use--

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.510
a lot of practical compressors
today use some version of ANS.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.090
So ANS is basically a class--

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:34.350
it's a class of compressors.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.210
It's not really a
single compressor.

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:39.720
And for example, rANS
is one version, tANS--

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.179
r stands for range,
t stands for table.

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:45.960
These are like two extremes
of this family of compressors,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.740
but there are various
variants of ANS out there.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:53.430
And basically, they have
replaced Arithmetic coding

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.050
and Huffman coding
in various places

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.630
depending on the
application requirement.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:00.060
So for example,
rANS is basically

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.430
just a drop in replacement
for Arithmetic,

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.988
but it's going to be faster.

00:09:03.988 --> 00:09:06.030
So if you care about the
compression performance,

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.400
you might want to do
this while getting

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.930
advantages over the speed.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:14.040
Similarly, tANS is actually a
drop in replacement for Huffman.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.700
But now, in its case, it's
almost as fast as Huffman,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:22.180
but it gets much
better compression.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.970
So it's like depending
on your application,

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.720
these algorithms are
directly driven to be--

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:34.020
they have the best properties
of Huffman or Arithmetic.

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:34.520
OK.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.380
So now that we
understand like this--

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:41.180
there is something missing
in the kind of code--

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.760
in kind of encoding strategies
which we have learned

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.380
and there exists
something, let's try

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:48.620
to understand it a little
better like, how it works.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.450
So this has a very funky name.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:53.180
It's called asymmetric
numeral system,

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:55.820
which in itself is a good
question, like why do you call

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.310
it asymmetric numeral system.

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:01.520
But even before we start with
asymmetric numeral system,

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.740
let's start with
asymmetric numeral system

00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:08.690
example, which actually all
of us have seen in real life.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:11.510
So suppose you have
inputs as digits, just

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.510
single digits, 0 to 9.

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:16.850
And let's say you
want to encode this.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:20.810
So some data stream, which
comes from the alphabet 0 to 9.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:24.740
So let's say your data
input is 3, 2, 4, 1, 5.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.090
OK.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:29.000
Can you form a single
number x, which

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.470
represents this data input?

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:35.890
I heard a yeah.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:36.430
Sorry.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:37.720
Yeah.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:38.710
How would you do that?

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:45.380
How would you represent-- if
you knew these were just digits,

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.600
how would you represent
this as a single state

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:52.600
and tell your friend
you would use this?

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:56.280
So the answer, you would use
it as a digit of one number.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.760
Exactly.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:03.090
You can encode
this as just 32415.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:07.680
And if you think about
what this 32415 really is--

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.900
what was the encoding
operation you did?

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.130
Can somebody answer that?

00:11:16.745 --> 00:11:17.245
Sorry?

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.300
Yeah.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:25.250
So the answer was use the base
10, which is exactly the idea

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.980
here like, you will use
base 10, but now you

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:31.700
have to think about your data
will be input as a stream rate.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.790
So we are looking into a
streaming kind of code.

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:40.070
So you would start with 3,
but exactly like you said,

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.290
we'll see how we can form this.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.060
So let's say you start with 3.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:47.660
So that was the only thing, you
would just represent it as 3.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:52.010
Now let's say you received
the second symbol 3 comma 2.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:57.230
So the way you would do this is
just multiply it with 10, add 2.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:58.340
That's 32.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:02.360
That's exactly the decimal
10 expansion of this thing.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.830
Now let's say the third
symbol you receive is 4.

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.230
So one way to think
of it is, again,

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:14.240
like decimal expansion, 3
times 100, 2 times 10 plus 4,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.170
but you could have also
thought of this as just 32

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:21.605
multiplied by 10 plus 4.

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:29.990
And does this give you an idea
of basically forming a streaming

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:31.835
encoder for this thing?

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:38.460
So the idea is very
simple, at each point,

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.500
you maintain some initial state.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.800
That's the only thing
you are maintaining.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:46.870
When you receive
the first input 3,

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:49.150
you just multiply the
previous state by 10

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:51.790
and add the new input.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:53.290
That's the only
thing we were doing.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.440
That's the decimal expansion
of, so as to say, this thing.

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.150
And you keep
updating your state.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.450
So when you receive 3,
your next state is 3.

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:05.230
After you receive 2, it's
32 and so on and so forth.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:09.040
So 320 plus 4, 3240 plus 1.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:16.200
And you keep doing that to
get the final state, 32415.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.400
Now suppose your friend comes
to you and gives you this state

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:23.100
and asks you, what
was the symbols

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.930
which were encoding-- encoded?

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:25.725
Can you do that?

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:34.530
Do exactly the next question.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:35.030
Yeah.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:41.150
Exactly.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:44.840
So the solution
which he gave was--

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.460
I can start--

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:52.920
I'll start with this, sorry,
32145, as my initial state.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:14:00.030
I'll divide it by 10 and
calculate the modulus operand.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.008
And this will give me--

00:14:02.008 --> 00:14:03.680
sorry.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:08.570
This will give me 32145.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:11.330
And then I can keep
repeating this operation.

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:15.530
So in this particular
case, the by 10

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:18.680
operation gives you
the previous state.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.780
So this was the previous
state from which

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:22.220
you got your new state.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:25.040
And the modulus operand
gives you the symbol.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:30.230
But now you see
something interesting.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:35.590
When you do that, you will
actually get 5 first, then 4,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:38.600
then 1, then 2, then 3.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:44.740
So basically, your decoding runs
in exactly the same fashion,

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:45.970
like we just talked about.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.850
OK, your decoding runs
in the exact same fashion

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:55.820
we just talked about.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:58.210
So you just do modulo 10.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:02.260
Sorry, you just find the integer
part as well as the remainder,

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:05.950
and you keep recovering back
the state, which you had,

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.930
and the symbol, which
was encoded the last.

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:13.570
So when you start with 32145,
you will first get a symbol as 5

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:15.580
and the previous state as 3214.

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:18.190
Again, you divide
this by 10, 3214.

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:20.170
You continue this.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:22.540
And you know where to
stop because either you

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:25.030
were told the number of
symbol or you get back

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:27.400
to some initial state
where you started from.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.760
So our encoding process started
from s equals to 0, you recover

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:34.240
that, and you can stop that, OK,
I got back to my initial state.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:39.690
OK, nice.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:43.650
So now if we were to
write this as a decoding

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:48.730
step and an encoding
step, what did we do?

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.020
We actually did nothing.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:56.360
So you are maintaining a state,
which you are going to output.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:57.822
Yeah, we are
maintaining a state,

00:15:57.822 --> 00:15:59.030
which we are going to output.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:01.880
Whenever you encode a symbol,
you multiply the previous state

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:05.830
by 10, and then you
added the symbol.

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:09.190
During decoding, you
start with some state x.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:13.660
You decode the symbol by first
taking the modulo operand.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:16.240
And then you update the
state by just dividing it,

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:19.750
by taking the
integer part by 10.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:21.970
And then your decode
step kind of needs

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:25.240
to return both the symbol
as well as the state

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:30.150
so that you can
continue this step.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:30.750
Nice.

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:35.490
And like we said,
in this procedure,

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:38.460
the symbols which you get
are in opposite order.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:43.140
So when you encode it, you
went from encoding 3 to 2 to 1

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:44.220
to 4 to 5.

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:46.920
But when you are decoding, you
are coming in opposite order.

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:52.030
And then each step,
you are able to recover

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:56.290
the exact same state, which
were used during the encoding.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:59.560
So when you went from
encoding, you went from--

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:02.440
if you just look at the state,
you went from 0 to 3 to 32

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:07.960
to 3214 to 32145, but the
decoding was a reverse process.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.240
You started with
32145 and each step

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:11.455
you recovered the same state.

00:17:15.219 --> 00:17:16.569
Are we clear?

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:17.069
All right.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.660
So far, very simple,
very simple idea.

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:27.700
All of us have used this in some
fashion one way or the other.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:30.670
So this is exactly like the
symmetric numeral system

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:32.440
encoder.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.450
There is one last piece missing.

00:17:34.450 --> 00:17:37.750
We never converted our
things to any bit arrays.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.210
We just left it at this
number, this state to be.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.930
Encoded So the last
step is basically just

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:48.010
transmitting the final state
x in binary using something

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:53.500
like ceil of log2 x plus 1 bits
and like similar idea as what

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.130
Shubham discussed last time.

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:58.600
And so if you now look at
the complete encoding step--

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:01.150
so remember this was
our base encoding

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:04.900
step, which was just multiplying
by 10 and adding the symbol.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:08.440
And then if you have a data
input, which you want to encode,

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:12.310
you will start with some initial
state, which was 0 in our case,

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:13.480
like when we started.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.550
And then for each
symbol in data input,

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:20.290
you just encode your symbol
and you update your state.

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:25.570
And finally, you just
output the binary form

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:28.770
of whatever your state is.

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:31.530
So at this point, we
have a complete encoder.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.710
It gives you a bitstream
out, and you can losslessly

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:36.930
use to recover the
original bitstream.

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.870
And then when you
are decoding, you

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:45.780
are just reversing each step.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.630
Encoder and decoder are
exact reverse of each other.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.490
So the decode
step, first of all,

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:53.970
you are converted into
some uint from the bits,

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:55.770
so that you get
your final state.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.870
And then, again, you
have your symbols.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:59.850
And for all your
symbols, you will

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.100
do this decode step
over the state,

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.430
returning your symbol
and state, and you

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:07.260
will append the
symbols in a list,

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:08.880
and you will keep doing that.

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.130
It's occurring in a
streaming fashion.

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.740
Finally, the thing which
you need to be careful about

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:15.360
is you need to
reverse the symbols.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.880
Otherwise, the order of symbols
in which you are returning

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:21.400
things would be wrong.

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:24.550
And now in this case, we have
an encoder and a decoder,

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.980
which are exactly mirror
image of each other.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:29.050
You can ensure that
they will always

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:31.510
do lossless encoding decoding.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:34.940
OK.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:37.280
So we have a
lossless compressor.

00:19:40.510 --> 00:19:41.010
OK.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:46.110
Even before we go there, even
before we go to the next slide,

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:50.100
any ideas about what
kind of compression rates

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.395
is this compressor
able to achieve?

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:06.720
So as a hint, let's just
look at the encoding step.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.190
What are you doing?

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.590
So your symbols are always in--

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:20.270
your symbols are always 1
of 0, 1 to, whatever, 9,

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.520
but what about x?

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.400
As you keep encoding more and
more and more and more symbols,

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:26.555
what's happening to x?

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:39.610
It's very simple, no tricks.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:40.450
It keeps on growing.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.710
It keeps on growing.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:42.580
By how much?

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.360
Approximately 10.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:48.160
Exactly.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:52.300
So now what you're doing is your
state is going to 10 times state

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:55.720
x at each step approximately.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.930
And your state is
continuously growing.

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:01.840
So this number x is always--

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:06.960
after a point, it's always
going to start dominating s.

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:13.410
So roughly, for each symbol,
the price you are paying

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.425
is to encode a number, which is
10 times the previous number.

00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:23.190
And so you are
paying roughly log 2

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:28.175
to the base 10 extra
bits per symbol.

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:35.240
Now this only happens once you
have encoded a few of them.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:36.990
The first few symbols,
this won't be true.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:40.320
But you'll reach that stage
very fast because your symbol is

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:43.770
just limited in 0 to 10, whereas
remember, in our example,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.750
your state keeps on--

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.040
just keeps on growing.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:52.470
And this is exactly the idea.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:56.690
So at each step, your state
x is just becoming 10 times

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:57.830
the previous.

00:21:57.830 --> 00:22:00.050
And so roughly per
symbol, you are

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:02.165
paying these many extra bits.

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:05.510
Correct?

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:12.710
And so in this particular
case, a compressor actually

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:18.200
is an optimal compressor for
symbols, which are in 0 to 9,

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:22.040
if we assume that the symbols
were uniformly distributed.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:26.950
Because no matter
what the symbol is you

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:29.020
are paying roughly lock to--

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:32.440
lock 10 to the base two
extra bits to encode it,

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:35.140
which will be the entropy
in this particular case.

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:40.540
Great.

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:43.630
So in some sense,
we have already--

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:46.240
we have found a very
nice compressor,

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:50.940
which works well for
uniformly distributed symbols.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:53.250
You can think of how
you can generalize it

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:57.390
from 10 to something else, like
it need not be just 0 to 9.

00:22:57.390 --> 00:23:00.060
Any other numeral
system would do for you.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:03.720
So yeah.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:04.920
So that's good.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:09.480
But this compressor is
only optimal if you have--

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:13.890
basically each of these
symbols have probability 0.1.

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:17.490
It's uniformly distributed.

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:19.860
Does anyone have
any guesses what

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:25.140
we can do if let's say this
was a non-uniform distribution?

00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:42.410
Any guesses?

00:23:42.410 --> 00:23:43.670
Why was this optimal?

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:45.680
Let's just see this again.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:46.850
Let's leave it here.

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:49.280
So it was optimal
because my state

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:52.850
was being multiplied by 10
at each step for each symbol,

00:23:52.850 --> 00:23:55.890
no matter what the symbol was.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:58.730
Why 10?

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:01.010
Why does-- what does this imply?

00:24:07.270 --> 00:24:12.160
So another way to think about
this is the extra price I'm

00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:15.460
paying at each step was this.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:23.450
And so far in the class,
over almost every class

00:24:23.450 --> 00:24:27.920
we have talked about, this is
like one thumb rule you live by.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:30.860
If you can get a compressor
which gives you lens

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:33.800
around log 2 1 by p,
you have done the job.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:38.930
That's like the best thing you
can do with this compressor.

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:41.930
So now with this thing
in mind, can somebody

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:46.070
take a guess, what would you
do if these symbols were,

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:50.990
I don't know, coming with like
0 was coming with probability 0

00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:55.400
or p0, 1 was coming
with probability p1,

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:56.210
so on and so forth?

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:15.880
Yeah, that's a good guess.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:18.640
So the guess which
was said here was

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:22.150
like, OK, maybe we want to
multiply by a smaller number.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:24.900
But why smaller why not bigger?

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:26.903
Let's start with--
yes, I do want

00:25:26.903 --> 00:25:28.320
you to multiply
by something else,

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:31.110
but what was this
10 really, right?

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:34.230
Again, it's not arbitrary.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:38.490
It's actually the value
of log of 1 over p.

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:41.520
Sorry, it's value of 1 over p.

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:44.820
So what you would
want to do for--

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:46.710
when you have
different probabilities

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.710
really is somehow
multiply-- have

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:53.100
some equation which can give
you maybe something like this.

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:02.750
And if you can do
that, then roughly

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.030
whenever you get a symbol ps--

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:07.490
we'll discuss the details
how exactly to do that,

00:26:07.490 --> 00:26:10.140
but let's see if we
understand the idea.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.200
And if you can do
that, then no matter

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:15.440
what symbol ps when
it gets encoded,

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:21.050
you end up paying an extra
price of just log of 1

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:27.330
by p approximately, which is
the best thing you can do.

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:37.630
Is everyone with me?

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:41.980
If we understand
this, we have at least

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:45.790
understood the basic idea of
the asymmetric numeral system

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:49.165
and what we are trying to
design, why it might work.

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:56.070
So again, instead of scaling
x as 10 times x previous,

00:26:56.070 --> 00:27:01.220
we want to scale it as something
like 1 over probability of s

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:01.895
at each step.

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:08.860
OK.

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:11.640
Is everyone with me?

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.140
Cool.

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:15.760
So now let's see how
exactly rANS does this.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:19.360
And to do that, let's
introduce some notations,

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.580
which will be useful.

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:23.740
So we'll consider
probabilities which

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:27.075
can be written in terms
of integer frequencies.

00:27:27.075 --> 00:27:29.200
What that means is like,
if you have a probability,

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:33.550
you can write it as frequency
as by some integer m.

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:36.010
Let's take an example
just to be clear.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:37.780
So let's say you
have alphabet, which

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:40.990
is 0, 1, 2, and your
probabilities, which are 3 by 8,

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:43.390
3 by 8, 2 by 8.

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:45.430
In this case, I'll
represent this

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:48.250
as a list of
frequencies, which is

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:57.640
3, 3, 2, which is just
taking the top terms here,

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:01.180
and M, which is the
common denominator 8.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:10.210
And we'll also-- as we have
seen I think even in Arithmetic

00:28:10.210 --> 00:28:14.680
coding and before, something
which is really nice is--

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:18.040
an object to keep track of
is the cumulative probability

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:18.940
distribution.

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.940
And we'll keep track of
something parallel of that here.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:26.290
So we'll keep track of
the cumulative frequencies

00:28:26.290 --> 00:28:29.060
essentially.

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:29.560
OK.

00:28:29.560 --> 00:28:32.500
So the cumulative frequencies
in our case is like--

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:39.580
so frequencies are
for A, 3, B, 3, C, 2.

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.550
And so our cumulative
frequency array--

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:43.910
is that visible?

00:28:43.910 --> 00:28:44.990
OK.

00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:47.020
We'll start from
0 plus you'll add

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:52.150
the previous one, 3, plus
your add the previous one, 6.

00:28:52.150 --> 00:28:55.120
So just summing the frequencies
up until that point.

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:58.840
So this object will
be useful as well.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:08.390
And so this is--

00:29:08.390 --> 00:29:10.100
so in code terms,
somebody was like,

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:13.340
they were not able to think how
do I write it in code terms.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:14.540
Like, OK, I understand.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:16.250
Approximately, I
need to multiply it

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:18.530
by 1 by probability s.

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:26.450
But as we were seeing in
symmetrical numeral system,

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:28.490
we'll basically do
the exact same steps.

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:30.920
So if you look at
the encode step,

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:32.780
it's going to be
the exactly same.

00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.670
You start with
some initial state

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:36.590
for each symbol in data input.

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:39.680
You run this base encode
step, which we are calling,

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.170
which takes in the previous
state and the symbol

00:29:42.170 --> 00:29:44.300
and updates the state.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:47.045
And finally, you are going to
return the binary form of this.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:52.990
And so the only thing
which has changed

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:57.340
is this, base encode step,
which was previously--

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.890
so previously, what was this?

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:06.275
So previously, it was just
10 times x plus s return x.

00:30:06.275 --> 00:30:09.410
Now we need to
update this thing.

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:16.580
So given this
notation, any ideas

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:20.420
what we can do to get this?

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:33.130
It's not that hard.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:34.390
Yeah, go for it.

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:38.410
Well, we can take the ceiling
of one of a probability s

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:40.660
and then just apply the
int, but I'm not sure

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.050
how you would decode that.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:43.620
Perfect.

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:49.080
So the answer was we can maybe
take the ceiling of fs over m

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:53.190
instead of probably
s, but we are not sure

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:54.420
how do we decode that.

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:57.570
That's a great point,
and we'll come to that.

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:01.920
But this is the base encode
step in case of rANS.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:04.530
And what I want you
guys to-- so this is--

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:05.700
I'm giving it to you.

