00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:47.450
And before we start with that,
let's have some observation.

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:51.140
So this is our base
encode step, again,

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:55.220
something multiplied
with M, which

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:59.920
was 10 plus some other term.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:01.600
So let me just
write it like that.

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:05.380
I'm going to call the first term
block ID and the second term

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:06.070
slot.

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:10.240
And so your block ID is
just x previous modulo--

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:12.820
sorry, not modulo, integer
division frequency s.

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:17.710
So just this term, that's your
block ID, and everything else

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:20.655
is slot, this is called slot.

00:38:20.655 --> 00:38:22.030
So these are just
technical term.

00:38:22.030 --> 00:38:25.360
You don't have to worry,
but this is a slot.

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:29.170
And so one way to think about
what we just wrote like,

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.930
the actual ANS formula, is
writing your state update

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:35.020
as block ID times
M plus slot, which

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:40.430
is very similar format of
what we had previously.

00:38:40.430 --> 00:38:42.200
But let's talk about this slot.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:44.075
What can you say
about this slot?

00:38:48.593 --> 00:38:51.970
It's between cumul s
and cumul s plus 1.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:52.540
Excellent.

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:57.580
So the suggestion I got was--
so if you look at the slot,

00:38:57.580 --> 00:39:06.285
so slot is just cumul s plus
x previous mod of frequency s.

00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:12.650
And so this term, since
it's a modulus operation,

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:14.990
it's always going to be
less than frequency s.

00:39:18.460 --> 00:39:25.500
And cumul s, also it's always
going to be greater than 0.

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:29.040
So actually we got a nice tight
little bound, which is-- sorry,

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:31.110
it's always going to be
less than frequency s.

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:33.930
And this term will always
also be greater than 0

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:37.760
because it's a
modulus operation.

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:43.340
So what we got was-- what we got
right now is slot is always less

00:39:43.340 --> 00:39:46.250
than-- actually, cumul
s plus frequency,

00:39:46.250 --> 00:39:49.340
I can just write it as
cumulative of the next symbol

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:52.580
because that's the
definition of the cumulative.

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:57.230
And it's always greater
than the cumulative of s.

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:03.980
Actually, there is
an even looser bound

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:07.550
here, which you can follow,
which is your cumul,

00:40:07.550 --> 00:40:13.580
the cumulative probabilities,
will always be less than M.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:15.500
And the maximum value
of the cumulative

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:18.410
will always be less than the
total sum of frequencies, which

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:22.160
you have, because it's always
going to miss the last symbol.

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:24.290
And it's always
going to be greater

00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:27.380
than equals to 0 because your
cumulative probability is

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:30.610
the sum of positive numbers.

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:34.960
So these are strictly
weaker than the one

00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:37.360
which we worked out before.

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:40.120
But now does this
give you a hint?

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:45.790
So now what we have
gotten to combining 0.1

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:58.990
and 0.2 is that your x is block
ID times M plus slot, where

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:10.980
slot we know is always going
to be between 0 and M. So

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:13.530
does this give a hint
of what we can do next?

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:16.410
How can we retrieve the
slot versus block ID?

00:41:20.390 --> 00:41:20.960
Exactly.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:24.990
So we'll divide by M.
And so your slots--

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:25.490
OK.

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:27.320
So again, all of
these are worked out

00:41:27.320 --> 00:41:30.330
in this slide, whatever we are
working together in the class.

00:41:30.330 --> 00:41:35.180
So your slot is between
0 and M. That basically

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:37.970
tells that I can actually--

00:41:37.970 --> 00:41:43.910
during the base decode step,
which we now have to work out,

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:48.230
I can actually recover
the block ID and slot

00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:54.200
by just taking the modulo
operation and the integer

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:57.160
part of division.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:41:59.890
And the only thing here
we used during decoding

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:03.640
was the fact that my
slot is always between 0

00:42:03.640 --> 00:42:07.600
and M and my encoding step was
block ID times M plus slot.

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:13.570
But we still haven't
recovered our symbol,

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:14.710
all the previous state.

00:42:14.710 --> 00:42:17.410
We just recovered
slot, which was

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:20.410
this whole big term,
cumulative of something

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:25.910
plus state modulo operation
with some frequency.

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:29.390
So any idea once I
know the block id--

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:34.120
so now I know the block
ID and I know the slot.

00:42:34.120 --> 00:42:37.360
How can I maybe recover
this symbol out of it?

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:47.240
So I know the value of
my slot, which is also

00:42:47.240 --> 00:42:53.390
equals to cumulative of s
plus this whole thing, x

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:56.496
previous modulo frequency s.

00:43:03.700 --> 00:43:04.480
Here's a hint.

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:09.880
We actually know
something more about slot.

00:43:09.880 --> 00:43:15.070
Slot is not only greater
than equals to 0 less than M,

00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:21.210
but actually it's always greater
than equals to cumulative of s.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:22.905
Sorry, let me just rewrite this.

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:33.350
And less than equals
to cumulative--

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:36.905
sorry, less than strictly
of cumulative of s plus 1.

00:43:40.110 --> 00:43:41.820
We just worked out
a tighter bound.

00:43:44.740 --> 00:43:49.020
Does this give you a hint
on how you can find the s?

00:43:56.840 --> 00:43:58.820
Again, none of these
questions are hard.

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:01.190
Feel free to throw out
whatever the first thing comes

00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:02.040
in your mind.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:02.540
It's OK.

00:44:09.310 --> 00:44:09.940
Yeah.

00:44:09.940 --> 00:44:10.480
OK.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:12.340
Anyone wants to build
up on that answer?

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.410
So for the first s, such that--

00:44:24.811 --> 00:44:31.260
the last s, such that cumulative
s is less than or equal to slot.

00:44:31.260 --> 00:44:34.140
Cumulative s is-- exactly.

00:44:34.140 --> 00:44:37.340
So basically, the
point is your cumul

00:44:37.340 --> 00:44:40.830
s is basically a monotonically
increasing array.

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:45.730
So what I'm showing here
is-- let's say this is--

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:47.500
actually, no, this is wrong.

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:50.930
I need to start from 0.

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:51.830
OK.

00:44:51.830 --> 00:44:53.490
So your cumul s--

00:44:57.610 --> 00:45:01.720
so this is, let's say,
symbol 0, symbol 1, symbol 2.

00:45:01.720 --> 00:45:04.300
At each point whenever
you encounter that symbol,

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:08.590
you are adding the
frequency of that symbol.

00:45:08.590 --> 00:45:10.960
And so once you know
that, I don't know,

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:16.840
slot basically is a number,
which is always between cumul

00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:18.730
of something, all
you need to find

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:23.080
is the band in which
the slot lies really.

00:45:23.080 --> 00:45:25.720
So we know that this is
an increasing function,

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:30.970
all you need to do is like find
me the s, such that your slot is

00:45:30.970 --> 00:45:33.610
actually greater than
the cumulative of that

00:45:33.610 --> 00:45:35.800
and less than the
cumulative of next one.

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:41.070
And cumulative actually
is a sorted array.

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:46.230
So this is where your computer
science search information

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:47.340
would also come into play.

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:49.680
You can do this
very efficiently.

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:53.520
But basically, the
step which you use is--

00:45:53.520 --> 00:45:57.120
so again, you use this hint
that your cumul s between this

00:45:57.120 --> 00:46:00.990
and all you can do is
do a binary search.

00:46:00.990 --> 00:46:04.710
All you need to do is like,
do a left biased binary search

00:46:04.710 --> 00:46:08.070
to find the symbol
s, which is-- such

00:46:08.070 --> 00:46:11.775
that cumul s is just less
than the slot, and then cumul

00:46:11.775 --> 00:46:13.665
s plus 1 is greater
than that slot.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:21.570
And because of this exact
property, which we worked out,

00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:23.500
this is going to be unique.

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:25.290
There's only going to
be one symbol, which

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:26.745
is going to return that.

00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:33.970
Are people with me?

00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:40.230
All right.

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:41.100
So are we done?

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:46.290
So now we know how to decode the
symbol, should we call it a day?

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:52.430
We need to know x_prev.

00:46:52.430 --> 00:46:54.770
We need to know
the previous state.

00:46:54.770 --> 00:46:57.110
As we know, this is a
