00:46:57.110 --> 00:47:00.320
We recovered our s, but we
also need to uniquely determine

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:02.840
what our previous state was.

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:04.280
Any ideas?

00:47:04.280 --> 00:47:11.130
So now by this point, by step
two, we know what our s is.

00:47:11.130 --> 00:47:12.210
Sorry.

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.640
We know what our s is.

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:22.020
We know the block ID,
which is x previous integer

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:24.930
division of frequency s.

00:47:24.930 --> 00:47:33.870
And we also know our slot,
just cumulative of s plus this.

00:47:33.870 --> 00:47:36.310
I'm not going to rewrite
this, but you know the modulus

00:47:36.310 --> 00:47:40.550
at this point.

00:47:40.550 --> 00:47:45.740
So can we work
out the x previous

00:47:45.740 --> 00:47:47.330
or do we need to
do something more?

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:53.528
if we multiply our
block ID by previous s,

00:47:53.528 --> 00:47:58.018
we get back to where we
would [INAUDIBLE] for missing

00:47:58.018 --> 00:47:58.518
our reader.

00:47:58.518 --> 00:48:02.471
So [INAUDIBLE] remainders in
our slot [INAUDIBLE] cumul

00:48:02.471 --> 00:48:06.910
s to get that remainder part,
get the whole [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:06.910 --> 00:48:07.660
Exactly.

00:48:07.660 --> 00:48:08.890
I'm not going to repeat.

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:11.402
I'm going to-- actually,
this is where I'm just

00:48:11.402 --> 00:48:12.610
going to give you the answer.

00:48:12.610 --> 00:48:15.070
It's basically
direct manipulation

00:48:15.070 --> 00:48:17.680
of terms at this point.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:22.780
All you need to do is
rearrange your original term.

00:48:22.780 --> 00:48:29.960
So remember, your
encoding was x equals

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:43.090
to x previous by frequency s
plus cumul s plus x previous mod

00:48:43.090 --> 00:48:43.982
frequency s.

00:48:48.565 --> 00:48:52.270
You know this term because you
have figured out the symbol s.

00:48:52.270 --> 00:48:54.820
So you know exactly
what cumul s is.

00:48:54.820 --> 00:48:59.050
So at this point, you know the
value of x previous modulus

00:48:59.050 --> 00:49:03.250
frequency is and you know the
value of x previous integer

00:49:03.250 --> 00:49:04.750
division of frequency is.

00:49:04.750 --> 00:49:07.300
So you can just rearrange
the terms to figure out

00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:09.740
what your x previous was.

00:49:09.740 --> 00:49:13.460
And this is what
it turns out to be.

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:16.040
So just at this point, you
are just rearranging the term

00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:17.960
and using the fact
that you know the s,

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:20.390
you know the exact
value of cumul s

00:49:20.390 --> 00:49:23.270
and you're just using
the initial equation

00:49:23.270 --> 00:49:24.395
to get back x previous.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.980
So great.

00:49:31.980 --> 00:49:34.860
So ideally, this should--

00:49:34.860 --> 00:49:39.240
while we were working it out, it
seems that it should work out.

00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:41.970
In the sense, we should just--

00:49:41.970 --> 00:49:44.670
we are just reversing
the steps very

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:47.400
similar to how we were doing in
the symmetric numeral systems.

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:50.040
We are just reversing
the steps and recovering

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:53.790
symbols in opposite order
and recovering the state.

00:49:53.790 --> 00:49:56.130
But let's work out an
example together just so

00:49:56.130 --> 00:50:00.120
that I feel confident
that you guys understand,

00:50:00.120 --> 00:50:03.990
even though it's just redoing
the same maths operation.

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:07.050
Personally, I feel just redoing
these algorithms by hand

00:50:07.050 --> 00:50:10.440
give me a lot of intuitions on
what's happening, even for maybe

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:12.040
simpler examples.

00:50:12.040 --> 00:50:15.820
So yeah, I encourage
you guys to do that.

00:50:15.820 --> 00:50:18.430
So remember like in
our previous case,

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:22.690
we worked out the x final was
this corresponding to some bit

00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:23.190
array.

00:50:23.190 --> 00:50:24.960
So it's like the
same running example

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:28.170
we are working with, where
I have three symbols A, B,

00:50:28.170 --> 00:50:32.260
C with probability 3 by
8, 3 by 8, and 2 by 8,

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:37.610
and the cumulative frequencies
for them is 0, 3, 6.

00:50:37.610 --> 00:50:39.980
And your final is 101.

00:50:39.980 --> 00:50:42.990
So let's try to work it out.

00:50:42.990 --> 00:50:45.060
Let's just do maybe
one step together,

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:48.210
and then I'll encourage
you guys to go home and do

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:49.680
the rest of them.

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:58.000
OK, so step one,
so x final is 101.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:02.050
So again, we'll just repeat the
steps here, step one, step two,

00:51:02.050 --> 00:51:05.360
step three like, the three
steps we worked out together.

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:05.860
OK.

00:51:05.860 --> 00:51:08.830
So step one is getting
the block ID and slot.

00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:14.560
So your block ID and slot
will just be division by 8

00:51:14.560 --> 00:51:16.600
because my M is 8.

00:51:16.600 --> 00:51:17.690
And let's see.

00:51:17.690 --> 00:51:22.060
So can somebody check that
your block ID would be 12

00:51:22.060 --> 00:51:23.200
and slot will be 5?

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:30.060
Yeah, seems about right.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:30.990
OK.

00:51:30.990 --> 00:51:32.820
So this is step one.

00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:37.410
Now step two says find
the symbol s, such

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:41.280
that my slot is in between
the cumul of the previous one,

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:43.650
and the next one.

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:45.870
So our cumul s array is this.

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:49.860
5 belongs in between 3
and 6, and so my symbol

00:51:49.860 --> 00:51:53.220
would be this guy,
the second one.

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:56.840
So my symbol is 1.

00:51:56.840 --> 00:52:02.360
And that also means my cumul
s, in this case, is just 3,

00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:06.110
which is just the
cumulative probability of 1.

00:52:06.110 --> 00:52:09.920
And then the step three is
just working out this math.

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:10.880
So let's just do it.

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:16.700
X previous is block ID, which
is 12, times frequency of s,

00:52:16.700 --> 00:52:19.040
3, plus slot.

00:52:19.040 --> 00:52:19.820
What's our slot?

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:22.610
5 minus cumul s 3.

00:52:22.610 --> 00:52:24.155
So this gives 38.

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:32.780
So the algorithm outputs--

00:52:32.780 --> 00:52:34.850
so let me maybe rewrite it.

00:52:38.050 --> 00:52:38.970
All right.

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:41.820
So you return the symbol
and previous state.

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:44.970
So your symbol return would be--

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:48.600
symbol is 1 and your
previous state is 38.

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:54.820
So now let's just--

00:52:54.820 --> 00:52:58.650
OK, we can continue
doing this, and I

00:52:58.650 --> 00:53:01.830
encourage you guys to go
home and continue doing this.

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:04.260
But just as a reminder--

00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:05.865
let's see where we
did the encoding.

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:17.460
OK, so this is where
we did the encoding.

00:53:17.460 --> 00:53:21.720
We just worked out the first
step going back from 101.

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:24.060
And the symbol we
outputted was one.

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:28.820
And the previous state
we outputted was 38.

00:53:28.820 --> 00:53:29.510
OK.

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:32.330
So you see it's like
the exact backtracking.

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:35.090
So the basic idea is, as
you keep adding symbols,

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:36.890
your state is increasing.

00:53:36.890 --> 00:53:41.720
And when you start decoding,
you start decreasing your state

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:46.570
and outputting back the
same symbol at each point.

00:53:46.570 --> 00:53:51.430
This was like, 1,
0, or sorry, 1021.

00:53:51.430 --> 00:53:53.580
And then while out
coming back, you

00:53:53.580 --> 00:53:55.885
will just output them
in reverse order.

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:11.310
So here I just
did only one step,

00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:14.310
but please go home, work out
this example, and ideally,

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:16.755
you should be able to recover
all the previous steps.

00:54:22.080 --> 00:54:23.310
Any questions?

00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:31.170
Asymmetric, as we said,
it was [INAUDIBLE]..

00:54:31.170 --> 00:54:34.480
Still, how is the encoding
key process so long

00:54:34.480 --> 00:54:35.480
to the exact same stage?

00:54:35.480 --> 00:54:37.610
So asymmetric was not for that.

00:54:37.610 --> 00:54:40.190
It was because of
numeral systems.

00:54:40.190 --> 00:54:43.520
So symmetric numeral system is
no matter what point you get,

00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:44.930
they are multiplying it by 10.

00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:46.760
In our case, the
thing which you are

00:54:46.760 --> 00:54:52.160
multiplying the previous
state with is very different.

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:54.650
It depends on the symbol,
which is coming in.

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:57.822
So if you were encoding 0
to 10 with very different

00:54:57.822 --> 00:54:59.780
probabilities, we'll be
multiplying each number

00:54:59.780 --> 00:55:01.070
by a very different amount.

00:55:01.070 --> 00:55:04.910
So that's kind of the
asymmetricity part of it.

00:55:04.910 --> 00:55:07.250
But actually, you need--

00:55:07.250 --> 00:55:09.500
this is a very important
point, which you brought out.

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:11.960
You need to ensure that
as you are going forward

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:15.470
and backward, you reproduce
the exact same state

00:55:15.470 --> 00:55:17.720
because that's the
real point which

00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:19.340
is ensuring the lawlessness.

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:30.310
OK.

00:55:30.310 --> 00:55:31.960
And so this is just--

00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:32.950
I've already opened it.

00:55:32.950 --> 00:55:33.783
This is just a link.

00:55:33.783 --> 00:55:39.130
So [INAUDIBLE],, whose name
we hear a lot, because he

00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:42.400
co-designed the course
with us at past.

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:45.370
He had written this
blog post on ANS.

00:55:45.370 --> 00:55:47.200
And this is just an interactive.

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:50.560
So if you just want to play
with some symbol counts

00:55:50.560 --> 00:55:53.830
and put string, see how
the state kind of expands,

00:55:53.830 --> 00:55:56.770
you can just go and play
with this kind of thing.

00:55:56.770 --> 00:56:00.490
So I guess, in
our case, it was--

00:56:00.490 --> 00:56:01.120
I forgot.

00:56:01.120 --> 00:56:09.160
One second, let's
just check it, 0102.

00:56:09.160 --> 00:56:14.870
Can somebody remind me quickly
what's the input string?

00:56:18.686 --> 00:56:20.680
1021.

00:56:20.680 --> 00:56:21.730
Oh yeah, 1021.

00:56:21.730 --> 00:56:22.300
Thanks.

00:56:22.300 --> 00:56:31.510
So let's just put in 1021, comma
0 comma 2 comma 1, and try it.

00:56:31.510 --> 00:56:34.960
And you see we recover the
states which we were seeing.

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:38.020
And you can play with different
symbols, different frequency.

00:56:38.020 --> 00:56:41.260
I would encourage you guys to
still work by hand, so that--

00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:43.990
but you can use this
code snippet interactive

00:56:43.990 --> 00:56:46.720
to see if you are working
out things right or not.

00:56:46.720 --> 00:56:49.840
And then when you decode it,
you start with some state.

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:52.750
So in our case--

00:56:52.750 --> 00:56:55.120
why is this not opening up?

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:02.870
Interesting.

00:57:02.870 --> 00:57:06.360
For some reason, it's not
allowing me to edit it.

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:06.860
OK.

00:57:06.860 --> 00:57:09.980
The point is you can
change the state here.

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:12.050
I think I'm on iPad, so
it's not allowing me.

00:57:12.050 --> 00:57:14.120
And you can input the
number of encoded symbols.

00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:16.550
And again, the output,
which you'll start getting,

00:57:16.550 --> 00:57:19.997
is the states in reverse
order and bits thrown out

00:57:19.997 --> 00:57:20.705
in reverse order.

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:24.320
Yeah.

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:28.310
So this is just a tool which
you guys can play with.

00:57:28.310 --> 00:57:29.870
The link is in the slides.

00:57:36.210 --> 00:57:38.150
Let's see.

00:57:38.150 --> 00:57:38.660
OK.

00:57:38.660 --> 00:57:41.742
So this is now just a
combination of everything,

00:57:41.742 --> 00:57:42.950
which we have learned so far.

00:57:42.950 --> 00:57:46.490
So just to summarize
it one last time,

00:57:46.490 --> 00:57:48.870
the encoder works like this.

00:57:48.870 --> 00:57:51.500
So you start with some
initial state for each symbol

00:57:51.500 --> 00:57:52.790
in the data input.

00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:57.830
You run some base encode step,
which takes in state and symbol

00:57:57.830 --> 00:58:00.440
and gives you updated state.

00:58:00.440 --> 00:58:02.210
And then the final
output of the encoder

00:58:02.210 --> 00:58:06.620
is this two binary, which just
converts it into a binary.

00:58:06.620 --> 00:58:13.250
And the base encode step does
this block ID times M plus slot.

00:58:13.250 --> 00:58:17.060
And then this is like
the combined decoder.

00:58:17.060 --> 00:58:18.740
And so at this
point, this is called

00:58:18.740 --> 00:58:21.470
range ANS encoder and decoder.

00:58:21.470 --> 00:58:22.940
These are the operations.

00:58:22.940 --> 00:58:26.330
So during decoding, you
just do the exact inverse.

00:58:26.330 --> 00:58:29.780
So you convert bits
to final state first.

00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:33.470
Then for the range in symbols,
you run the base encode step.

00:58:33.470 --> 00:58:35.600
Your base encode
step has to return

00:58:35.600 --> 00:58:37.860
the symbol and the
previous state,

00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:40.590
and we just talked about
how we can recover that.

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:43.650
And finally, you need
to return the reverse.

00:58:43.650 --> 00:58:45.900
This is important
that you are always

00:58:45.900 --> 00:58:47.220
decoding in the reverse order.

00:58:50.430 --> 00:58:50.930
OK.

00:58:54.300 --> 00:58:57.240
So now if you think about--

00:58:57.240 --> 00:58:59.700
how we motivated rANS?

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:01.200
The first question
which I asked you

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:03.360
when we moved from
symmetric numeral systems

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:06.990
to asymmetric numeral
systems is, what's

00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:08.970
happening with the state?

00:59:08.970 --> 00:59:11.820
So again, with same
argument, your state

00:59:11.820 --> 00:59:16.290
now is increasing
by approximately 1

00:59:16.290 --> 00:59:18.780
over probability
of your symbol s

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:20.670
as your states keep increasing.

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:23.160
And so the final state
can be represented

00:59:23.160 --> 00:59:29.140
using approximately
log2 of x final bits.

00:59:29.140 --> 00:59:31.560
Correct?

00:59:31.560 --> 00:59:33.210
And so the question
for you guys is,

00:59:33.210 --> 00:59:36.000
what's the approximate encode
length for an input sn?

00:59:45.090 --> 00:59:49.410
So now suppose you have a
stream of symbols, s1, s2,

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:53.520
sn, which was like,
1, 0, 2, 1 before,

00:59:53.520 --> 00:59:57.570
what's the encode
length approximately?

01:00:04.472 --> 01:00:08.909
It would basically be the
expected value of 1, 2--

01:00:08.909 --> 01:00:12.880
1 over [? ps ?] [INAUDIBLE].

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:13.480
Exactly.

01:00:13.480 --> 01:00:13.980
Right.

01:00:13.980 --> 01:00:16.330
So now basically
for each symbol,

01:00:16.330 --> 01:00:19.690
as you are encoding sn
even without block lengths,

01:00:19.690 --> 01:00:23.020
so as to say involved like we
were seeing in Huffman code,

01:00:23.020 --> 01:00:25.660
you are basically
multiplying it--

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:27.520
let me write it with this.

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:30.070
Yeah, you are
multiplying first symbol.

01:00:30.070 --> 01:00:34.780
When first symbol comes, you
are multiplying x by 1 by ps1.

01:00:34.780 --> 01:00:38.320
Second symbol comes, you are
multiplying it by ps2 roughly,

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:40.510
given as 1 so on and so forth.

01:00:40.510 --> 01:00:44.200
And so finally,
the rough length,

01:00:44.200 --> 01:00:46.090
which you get--
which you will get,

01:00:46.090 --> 01:00:50.530
is you are multiplying the whole
symbol by 1 over probability

01:00:50.530 --> 01:00:53.440
of the n-tuple of
whatever that symbol is,

01:00:53.440 --> 01:00:57.450
and so your length is
just log inverse of this.

01:00:57.450 --> 01:00:59.850
And again, tying back
to our thumb rule,

01:00:59.850 --> 01:01:03.240
this basically tells
us, oh, we have suddenly

01:01:03.240 --> 01:01:06.450
found a very good
encoder decoder

01:01:06.450 --> 01:01:08.790
because it gives you something
very close to entropy.

01:01:11.630 --> 01:01:14.180
And so we are we are
not talking about--

01:01:14.180 --> 01:01:16.250
so this is all a very
hand-wavy argument,

01:01:16.250 --> 01:01:17.990
I know, but this is
more like, I guess,

01:01:17.990 --> 01:01:21.240
in the spirit of the
course for those of you

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:25.960
who are like mathematically
enthusiastic about how

01:01:25.960 --> 01:01:31.240
to get very formal bounce for
ANS like, we have been doing

01:01:31.240 --> 01:01:33.640
for arithmetic coding,
where we saw that there was

01:01:33.640 --> 01:01:36.790
a two-bit overhead over whatever
the block length of your symbol

01:01:36.790 --> 01:01:41.650
was or, in the case of
Huffman, where it was 1.

01:01:41.650 --> 01:01:45.383
We can discuss it offline, but
it's not trivial to analyze.

01:01:45.383 --> 01:01:47.050
What I really want
you guys to take away

01:01:47.050 --> 01:01:50.230
from this whole discussion
is that intuitively, it

01:01:50.230 --> 01:01:55.560
makes sense that this
compressor is somewhat optimal.

01:01:55.560 --> 01:01:57.570
It can give you good
compression rates

01:01:57.570 --> 01:01:59.310
like Arithmetic
coding was giving.

01:01:59.310 --> 01:02:01.530
This was roughly the
hand-wavy argument,

01:02:01.530 --> 01:02:03.240
even in the case of
Arithmetic coding.

01:02:11.840 --> 01:02:12.410
OK.

01:02:12.410 --> 01:02:16.370
And something very interesting
actually which we'll--

01:02:16.370 --> 01:02:20.750
just to connect it to what you
guys will see in future is--

01:02:20.750 --> 01:02:23.720
so I think when I first saw it--

01:02:23.720 --> 01:02:26.570
for most of the people I know,
when they first saw rANS,

01:02:26.570 --> 01:02:29.270
they had probably the same
reaction as some of you

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:31.220
like, where did
that equation come

01:02:31.220 --> 01:02:35.510
from, why does that cumul
s plus mod blah, blah,

01:02:35.510 --> 01:02:39.020
and then you somehow beautifully
come up with decoding mechanism

01:02:39.020 --> 01:02:40.640
where everything works out.

01:02:40.640 --> 01:02:43.610
You just had those
somehow some bonds,

01:02:43.610 --> 01:02:47.240
which kind of solve everything.

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:49.640
In this discussion,
we went over--

01:02:49.640 --> 01:02:51.290
I continuously gave
you hints, but now

01:02:51.290 --> 01:02:53.450
imagine you just somehow
surprisingly came up

01:02:53.450 --> 01:02:58.520
with that one day to a
friend and say this works.

01:02:58.520 --> 01:03:01.760
And historically, that's
somewhat how it happened.

01:03:01.760 --> 01:03:04.610
Somebody-- yeah, like
[? Duda, ?] actually,

01:03:04.610 --> 01:03:06.500
who's amazing, he came
up with this approach.

01:03:06.500 --> 01:03:08.900
And it took people a lot of
time to really understand

01:03:08.900 --> 01:03:10.880
what's really happening.

01:03:10.880 --> 01:03:13.710
But one actually very
cute, I would say, way

01:03:13.710 --> 01:03:16.260
to connect back to
what's really happening

01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:19.650
is this idea of
bits back coding.

01:03:19.650 --> 01:03:21.390
And we'll not
discuss it right now.

01:03:21.390 --> 01:03:26.230
It's just something I want
you guys to just know about.

01:03:26.230 --> 01:03:29.160
So this is like another
way to think about rANS.

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:31.230
So we took one
approach, but you could

01:03:31.230 --> 01:03:33.120
have taken a completely
different approach

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:36.150
and would have basically
landed at very similar

01:03:36.150 --> 01:03:40.350
looking equations,
and it basically

01:03:40.350 --> 01:03:43.470
has connection with this
joint entropy formula.

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:47.910
So if you were to
compress x ideally,

01:03:47.910 --> 01:03:50.700
one way to think about
this joint entropy formula,

01:03:50.700 --> 01:03:53.220
so this is for any
other random variable z.

01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:54.660
This is just an identity.

01:03:54.660 --> 01:03:56.980
This is a mathematical fact.

01:03:56.980 --> 01:03:59.880
You can think of
encoding and decoding

01:03:59.880 --> 01:04:03.900
x as like doing something with
joint distribution of x and z,

01:04:03.900 --> 01:04:07.020
and then doing something with
the conditional distribution

01:04:07.020 --> 01:04:08.990
of z and x.

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:11.840
And I know it's not
making sense right now.

01:04:11.840 --> 01:04:15.260
But basically this
minus sign, you

01:04:15.260 --> 01:04:19.220
can encode a symbol x using
some encoder decoder pair

01:04:19.220 --> 01:04:22.790
one, and some encoder
decoder pair two,

01:04:22.790 --> 01:04:26.090
where you have some minus
sign, which we haven't really

01:04:26.090 --> 01:04:26.875
dealt with.

01:04:26.875 --> 01:04:29.000
Everywhere we have been
dealing with positive lens,

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:30.590
positive everything.

01:04:30.590 --> 01:04:34.430
This kind of gives
the name bits back

01:04:34.430 --> 01:04:36.410
just like you will
encode something,

01:04:36.410 --> 01:04:38.630
but then you'll also
give some bits back

01:04:38.630 --> 01:04:42.390
to remain like close
to very ideal thing.

01:04:42.390 --> 01:04:44.880
And these all might
sound mysterious,

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:50.370
but go check out homework
2 Q4 hopefully soon.

01:04:50.370 --> 01:04:52.800
Less than 24 hours, you
should be able to see it.

01:04:52.800 --> 01:04:54.510
Work it out.

01:04:54.510 --> 01:04:59.340
And then for those of
you who are a bit--

01:04:59.340 --> 01:05:01.410
didn't appreciate the
fact like, what does it

01:05:01.410 --> 01:05:05.160
mean to have a conditional
entropy deeply,

01:05:05.160 --> 01:05:08.850
well, like next few lectures,
as Shubham pointed out even

01:05:08.850 --> 01:05:10.890
in the next class,
it's going to be--

01:05:10.890 --> 01:05:12.390
like, now we are
going to be talking

01:05:12.390 --> 01:05:14.610
about conditional entropy,
conditional probability

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:19.910
distributions because we'll
stop assuming independence.

01:05:19.910 --> 01:05:22.610
So basically, next few lectures
and working through homework

01:05:22.610 --> 01:05:24.410
will really make you
understand whatever

01:05:24.410 --> 01:05:29.400
we did today from another
light, independently.

01:05:29.400 --> 01:05:29.900
OK.

01:05:29.900 --> 01:05:35.510
So at this point, I want to go
into some qualitative aspects

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:37.230
instead of everything.

01:05:37.230 --> 01:05:40.220
So some properties of
rANS like, now where

01:05:40.220 --> 01:05:43.670
does rANS stand with respect
to arithmetic coding.

01:05:43.670 --> 01:05:46.730
So first of all, we understood
that the performance is optimal

01:05:46.730 --> 01:05:48.770
and actually very similar
to Arithmetic coding,

01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:52.310
goes back to our rule of thumb,
which we just talked about.

01:05:52.310 --> 01:05:54.860
We are actually doing
reverse decoding.

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:59.330
This can be a lot of problem
for streaming algorithms,

01:05:59.330 --> 01:06:00.290
if you think about it.

01:06:00.290 --> 01:06:02.570
That's why I've been
highlighting like reverse

01:06:02.570 --> 01:06:06.950
decoding because it's no more--

01:06:06.950 --> 01:06:08.542
the encoding is in
streaming fashion,

01:06:08.542 --> 01:06:10.250
let's say, but decoding
now will give you

01:06:10.250 --> 01:06:14.120
symbols in opposite direction,
which might not be good

01:06:14.120 --> 01:06:17.900
if a low latency or a high
throughput encoder decoder pair

01:06:17.900 --> 01:06:19.250
is your demand.

01:06:19.250 --> 01:06:23.060
But you can see in work, there
are schemes to work around it.

01:06:23.060 --> 01:06:26.780
And it actually has simpler
encoding and decoding.

01:06:26.780 --> 01:06:29.540
So this is something which
we didn't really talk about,

01:06:29.540 --> 01:06:33.620
but Shubham mentioned last
class like Arithmetic coding

01:06:33.620 --> 01:06:36.620
is amazing, but it has
these floating point

01:06:36.620 --> 01:06:39.380
operations involved everywhere.

01:06:39.380 --> 01:06:45.380
Whereas in our encoder--

01:06:45.380 --> 01:06:48.440
I'm just going back
to our rANS encoder.

01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:50.540
Actually, you have
some operations which

01:06:50.540 --> 01:06:52.070
can be done very efficiently.

01:06:52.070 --> 01:06:55.730
So for example, even though
you have integer division,

01:06:55.730 --> 01:06:58.280
you can pre-compute
1 over frequency s.

01:06:58.280 --> 01:07:01.400
So you don't-- like this is just
like this can be cached and done

01:07:01.400 --> 01:07:02.090
once.

01:07:02.090 --> 01:07:05.990
Similarly, you can just be
intelligent about the choice

01:07:05.990 --> 01:07:10.040
of M. So if M is a power
of two, multiplying by it

01:07:10.040 --> 01:07:13.670
is just a left shift, which can
be done extremely efficiently

01:07:13.670 --> 01:07:14.720
in hardware.

01:07:14.720 --> 01:07:17.750
So basically choosing
engineering choices

01:07:17.750 --> 01:07:20.150
gives you a lot of
efficiency in terms

01:07:20.150 --> 01:07:23.810
of how rANS can be implemented
and is the reason why it

01:07:23.810 --> 01:07:26.735
has encoding decoding faster.

01:07:30.560 --> 01:07:34.250
But there is actually one issue
which we still haven't solved.

01:07:34.250 --> 01:07:36.590
So rANS sounds great.

01:07:36.590 --> 01:07:40.880
Any guesses practically, what
might be one of the issues?

01:07:48.720 --> 01:07:49.516
Yeah?

01:07:49.516 --> 01:07:55.250
To define that that works
with the [INAUDIBLE]..

01:07:55.250 --> 01:07:55.750
Yeah.

01:07:55.750 --> 01:07:58.270
So that's probably
a comment, which

01:07:58.270 --> 01:08:00.430
is coming after
attempting homework 1.

01:08:00.430 --> 01:08:04.060
Yes, you do have to find
M, which works well.

01:08:04.060 --> 01:08:06.860
But I would say that
isn't fundamental issue.

01:08:06.860 --> 01:08:07.840
It can be worked out.

01:08:07.840 --> 01:08:10.600
Take M large enough for like--

01:08:10.600 --> 01:08:12.490
probabilities are never exact.

01:08:12.490 --> 01:08:14.050
Let's say, for
whatever precision

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:16.510
you are working
with maybe it's OK,

01:08:16.510 --> 01:08:18.520
but there is actually
the fundamental issue.

01:08:18.520 --> 01:08:22.000
So if you look at like
the input symbol string,

01:08:22.000 --> 01:08:28.330
as it keeps growing, your state
just never stops increasing.

01:08:28.330 --> 01:08:30.040
You are always
increasing your state,

01:08:30.040 --> 01:08:34.430
you are always multiplying
it with something.

01:08:34.430 --> 01:08:39.020
And so no matter what you
do after a few symbols,

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:41.660
when I say a few,
maybe around 20 to 40,

01:08:41.660 --> 01:08:43.520
you will already
reach a state which

01:08:43.520 --> 01:08:46.790
will start overflowing
no matter what you do.

01:08:46.790 --> 01:08:47.630
And this is like--

01:08:47.630 --> 01:08:51.470
I would say, this
has a parallel to--

01:08:51.470 --> 01:08:52.040
OK.

01:08:52.040 --> 01:08:55.340
Yeah, so this has a
parallel to what we learned

01:08:55.340 --> 01:08:57.135
in Arithmetic coding also.

01:08:57.135 --> 01:08:58.760
So as you keep
multiplying probability,

01:08:58.760 --> 01:09:01.100
your intervals keep
becoming small and small.

01:09:01.100 --> 01:09:03.500
And a lot of
practical compressor

01:09:03.500 --> 01:09:06.620
goes into a lot of depth
into solving these issues,

01:09:06.620 --> 01:09:09.065
because these are very
important and practical issues.

01:09:13.689 --> 01:09:18.229
And so yeah, basically after 30,
40 symbols, you will go above.

01:09:18.229 --> 01:09:18.729
OK.

01:09:18.729 --> 01:09:22.029
So one solution to avoid this
is like you can restrict the x

01:09:22.029 --> 01:09:25.720
to be an interval L comma
H. One thing which you

01:09:25.720 --> 01:09:28.660
don't-- wouldn't want
to do is like restart.

01:09:28.660 --> 01:09:31.300
You wouldn't want to be like,
OK, whenever it overflows,

01:09:31.300 --> 01:09:33.970
I reset my state and I
start from the beginning,

01:09:33.970 --> 01:09:36.310
because that's losing
all the advantage.

01:09:36.310 --> 01:09:37.779
It's losing the block advantage.

01:09:37.779 --> 01:09:39.460
It's losing the fact
that states need

01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:41.380
to be high enough
to actually get

01:09:41.380 --> 01:09:45.069
you a compression ratio
near the log probability,

01:09:45.069 --> 01:09:48.000
log 1 over probability.

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:50.460
And so one way to
solve this is--

01:09:50.460 --> 01:09:54.490
so we'll not think--

01:09:54.490 --> 01:09:57.370
I don't want to get into
the details given the time,

01:09:57.370 --> 01:10:01.660
but basically, now
you will try to keep

01:10:01.660 --> 01:10:06.610
the state in between
this interval L comma H.

01:10:06.610 --> 01:10:10.790
And one way to do this is,
when you do the encode step,

01:10:10.790 --> 01:10:13.520
your first step is--

01:10:13.520 --> 01:10:16.460
even your base
encode step always

01:10:16.460 --> 01:10:20.370
assumes the state, which you are
getting, is in between L and H.

01:10:20.370 --> 01:10:22.830
So before actually
calling this step,

01:10:22.830 --> 01:10:26.640
you need to call a shrink
state step, which takes some

01:10:26.640 --> 01:10:30.090
x and returns the
state x shrunk, which

01:10:30.090 --> 01:10:34.790
is in between some
predefined window, L comma H.

01:10:34.790 --> 01:10:38.300
But obviously, this step will
be lossy if you don't actually

01:10:38.300 --> 01:10:39.890
give out some bits with it.

01:10:39.890 --> 01:10:41.600
Because you're
changing the state,

01:10:41.600 --> 01:10:44.510
you're just dividing it and
putting it to some other thing,

01:10:44.510 --> 01:10:46.010
you are losing some information.

01:10:46.010 --> 01:10:48.395
So this step also
gives out some bits.

01:10:54.310 --> 01:10:54.810
Yeah.

01:10:54.810 --> 01:10:56.550
Let's not go into the details.

01:10:56.550 --> 01:10:59.370
Let me just show you like
how this kind of function

01:10:59.370 --> 01:11:00.310
looks like.

01:11:00.310 --> 01:11:07.930
So if you start with x previous
something like 22 or 1011b,

01:11:07.930 --> 01:11:11.890
one way to get this string state
is to always divide this by 2

01:11:11.890 --> 01:11:15.210
and output the last bit.

01:11:15.210 --> 01:11:19.800
So for example, if you start
with 10110, at first step,

01:11:19.800 --> 01:11:24.120
you can do 1011, and then
you can out the last bit 0.

01:11:24.120 --> 01:11:27.270
Next step, you can
output 1, you can

01:11:27.270 --> 01:11:31.440
take out 1 and add it
to the list of out bits

01:11:31.440 --> 01:11:33.540
and keep it to 101.

01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:36.720
And you keep doing this
until your state basically

01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:46.790
comes in the interval L comma
H. And this is basically

01:11:46.790 --> 01:11:51.530
your string state function,
which is while your base encode

01:11:51.530 --> 01:11:55.640
step is not in this
interval, you just

01:11:55.640 --> 01:11:59.270
maintain an out bits, which
is just modulo base 2,

01:11:59.270 --> 01:12:01.880
and you keep shrinking
your original range.

01:12:04.400 --> 01:12:04.900
OK.

01:12:04.900 --> 01:12:07.810
So what it's doing
is that at each step,

01:12:07.810 --> 01:12:09.190
I might output some bits.

01:12:09.190 --> 01:12:13.210
I'm not really waiting to encode
the whole state towards the end.

01:12:13.210 --> 01:12:15.700
And at that point,
I output some bit.

01:12:15.700 --> 01:12:17.530
That's really the
key difference.

01:12:21.270 --> 01:12:23.190
And then a really
important question

01:12:23.190 --> 01:12:27.300
to think about is, how many
bits do you have to stream out?

01:12:27.300 --> 01:12:30.390
What is this L comma H?

01:12:30.390 --> 01:12:34.810
And in practice, you need
to ensure two things.

01:12:34.810 --> 01:12:37.540
Whenever you are
shrinking this state,

01:12:37.540 --> 01:12:40.480
you need to ensure that
there is uniqueness involved.

01:12:40.480 --> 01:12:41.770
There is only one--

01:12:41.770 --> 01:12:45.040
there is one, first of
all, and only one symbol,

01:12:45.040 --> 01:12:47.800
which is going to
lie given no matter

01:12:47.800 --> 01:12:51.140
what state you started with
to get between this L comma H

01:12:51.140 --> 01:12:51.640
window.

01:12:54.790 --> 01:12:57.970
And don't worry about it,
that basically gives you

01:12:57.970 --> 01:13:01.600
some bound about what
your L comma H can be,

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:03.280
and this was like
what was chosen

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:05.410
in the original paper of ANS.

01:13:05.410 --> 01:13:09.760
And choosing something like
this satisfies these condition.

01:13:13.790 --> 01:13:16.220
And I'm not going to
cover it in class.

01:13:16.220 --> 01:13:19.400
I think in the interest of
time, I don't want to go over.

01:13:19.400 --> 01:13:21.800
But all of these are also
present in the notes,

01:13:21.800 --> 01:13:23.540
so I would encourage
you guys to read

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:26.870
the notes to understand like
the basic idea, which we just

01:13:26.870 --> 01:13:27.710
discussed.

01:13:31.440 --> 01:13:33.060
But you might imagine
one thing which

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:36.630
I'll say since we have almost
discussed the idea like, so--

01:13:39.510 --> 01:13:40.010
come on.

01:13:44.450 --> 01:13:44.960
OK.

01:13:44.960 --> 01:13:48.260
So the full decoder
now will involve

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:50.990
some expanding of state
because right now you

01:13:50.990 --> 01:13:52.250
are shrinking some state.

01:13:52.250 --> 01:13:54.080
So when you are
decoding, you need

01:13:54.080 --> 01:13:57.650
to somehow expand that state
to get back the original thing,

01:13:57.650 --> 01:14:00.680
and that will use the out
bits, which were involved.

01:14:00.680 --> 01:14:04.220
So you will have some x
shrunk, some encoded bit array,

01:14:04.220 --> 01:14:08.390
and a reverse operation
of whatever we saw.

01:14:08.390 --> 01:14:10.550
And so your decode
step would-- it's

01:14:10.550 --> 01:14:12.560
going to be exact
opposite, same spirit.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:17.120
So your first step would be best
decode step, which will give you

01:14:17.120 --> 01:14:19.010
some state and shrunk
state, and then you

01:14:19.010 --> 01:14:23.830
will expand that state, which is
going to do a reverse operation.

01:14:23.830 --> 01:14:26.110
And combining
these two basically

01:14:26.110 --> 01:14:30.400
gives you a full streaming
encoder and decoder.

01:14:30.400 --> 01:14:30.900
OK.

01:14:30.900 --> 01:14:33.780
This is like the complete thing.

01:14:33.780 --> 01:14:35.940
A couple of things which
happens in practice again,

01:14:35.940 --> 01:14:38.430
is like you'll take
M to be power of two,

01:14:38.430 --> 01:14:41.100
you'll choose t to be
as large as possible

01:14:41.100 --> 01:14:43.770
when streaming
out this bits, you

01:14:43.770 --> 01:14:45.780
might not want to do a
single bit at a time,

01:14:45.780 --> 01:14:48.660
but bytes at a time.

01:14:48.660 --> 01:14:51.990
And we have the--

01:14:51.990 --> 01:14:55.740
so in SQL, we have
the rANS implemented.

01:14:55.740 --> 01:14:58.680
And again, all the
previous lectures,

01:14:58.680 --> 01:15:01.560
I would encourage you
guys to go over the code,

01:15:01.560 --> 01:15:05.130
see if you understand it.

01:15:05.130 --> 01:15:05.670
Yeah.

01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:09.240
And we didn't really go
over tANS in this lecture,

01:15:09.240 --> 01:15:13.070
but that's really like--

01:15:13.070 --> 01:15:16.810
the basic idea in tANS is
how can we speed this up.

01:15:16.810 --> 01:15:21.280
So the name of
tANS is table ANS.

01:15:21.280 --> 01:15:24.670
And so the question really
is, what else can we cache?

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:28.670
How can we really
speed this rANS up?

01:15:28.670 --> 01:15:32.540
Any ideas given homework 1,
what we have seen so far?

01:15:32.540 --> 01:15:34.225
How do you like--
what do you cache?

01:15:34.225 --> 01:15:35.225
How do you cache things?

01:15:41.650 --> 01:15:44.110
One recurring theme, which is--

01:15:44.110 --> 01:15:47.380
which we have seen maybe
even in three base codes

01:15:47.380 --> 01:15:52.660
is threes are slow because
you have to take decisions.

01:15:52.660 --> 01:15:55.600
And so whenever you have
to speed up something,

01:15:55.600 --> 01:15:59.650
you might want to come up
with some lookup tables.

01:15:59.650 --> 01:16:02.950
That's like a very,
very good rule of thumb

01:16:02.950 --> 01:16:03.858
when you are coding.

01:16:03.858 --> 01:16:05.650
If you can come up with
some lookup tables,

01:16:05.650 --> 01:16:07.900
you can really
speed up your code.

01:16:07.900 --> 01:16:14.980
And that's basically the idea
of caching rANS and tANS.

01:16:14.980 --> 01:16:17.620
And it kind of
relies on the fact

01:16:17.620 --> 01:16:23.380
that in our rANS base
encode step, when

01:16:23.380 --> 01:16:28.000
you go from this state
symbol to a new state,

01:16:28.000 --> 01:16:31.420
you are always maintaining
it between the same lower

01:16:31.420 --> 01:16:32.800
and higher window.

01:16:32.800 --> 01:16:36.250
So you can basically
pre-compute given

01:16:36.250 --> 01:16:38.380
whatever your previous
state is going to be,

01:16:38.380 --> 01:16:40.210
whatever new alphabet
is going to be,

01:16:40.210 --> 01:16:43.040
what your new state ever can be.

01:16:43.040 --> 01:16:46.700
And so you actually never
even have to compute that.

01:16:46.700 --> 01:16:49.250
And that's called
tANS and it basically

01:16:49.250 --> 01:16:53.270
takes some hit in
terms of performance.

01:16:53.270 --> 01:16:56.690
But the key idea really is that
lookup tables are really fast,

01:16:56.690 --> 01:17:02.070
so use them for
speed trade-offs.

01:17:02.070 --> 01:17:07.470
And so this is the last
slide, but this was also

01:17:07.470 --> 01:17:09.510
the first slide where
we started from.

01:17:09.510 --> 01:17:13.350
So again, combining all
the ideas which we learned,

01:17:13.350 --> 01:17:15.660
so I hope now you
guys understand

01:17:15.660 --> 01:17:18.150
exactly how rANS works.

01:17:18.150 --> 01:17:20.730
TANS we didn't go in details,
but we have some notes.

01:17:20.730 --> 01:17:22.680
You are welcome to
go through the notes.

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:25.230
You are welcome to go
through the code in SCL

01:17:25.230 --> 01:17:28.650
and come talk to us if you
want to understand that better.

01:17:28.650 --> 01:17:31.320
Variants of rANS
and tANS could also

01:17:31.320 --> 01:17:35.460
be interesting project proposals
and ideas to be implemented

01:17:35.460 --> 01:17:38.010
for practical compressors.

01:17:38.010 --> 01:17:40.620
These have really replaced
Arithmetic and Huffman

01:17:40.620 --> 01:17:44.940
coding in almost many new
modern compressors, which

01:17:44.940 --> 01:17:46.640
have come out.

01:17:46.640 --> 01:17:50.320
And the things to,
again, notice is

01:17:50.320 --> 01:17:54.610
that the compression performance
of rANS is very similar to tANS.

01:17:54.610 --> 01:17:56.500
And that's because of
that rule of thumb,

01:17:56.500 --> 01:17:58.390
which we have been
looking into and which we

01:17:58.390 --> 01:18:00.490
were able to achieve with rANS.

01:18:00.490 --> 01:18:04.150
tANS takes some hit
because we cache

01:18:04.150 --> 01:18:05.920
and we don't do
exact operations.

01:18:05.920 --> 01:18:09.550
But on the other hand,
if you look at speed,

01:18:09.550 --> 01:18:13.300
tANS has speeds, which are very
like-- at least decode speeds,

01:18:13.300 --> 01:18:15.490
which are very close to
Huffman and then encode,

01:18:15.490 --> 01:18:17.890
which is competitive
at the very least.

01:18:23.260 --> 01:18:24.700
Yeah.

01:18:24.700 --> 01:18:28.900
This was, I would
say, the last lecture

01:18:28.900 --> 01:18:31.960
in the series of
where we started--

01:18:31.960 --> 01:18:35.950
if we look back, we started this
class with very simple examples,

01:18:35.950 --> 01:18:37.270
IID.

01:18:37.270 --> 01:18:39.370
We worked out how
you can encode them

01:18:39.370 --> 01:18:42.520
to how you can encode
them optimally,

01:18:42.520 --> 01:18:45.250
like how you can encode them
losslessly to how you can encode

01:18:45.250 --> 01:18:47.290
them optimally losslessly.

01:18:47.290 --> 01:18:50.470
And then we learned various
different practical lossless

01:18:50.470 --> 01:18:53.140
codecs, really like various
different approaches,

01:18:53.140 --> 01:18:56.140
Huffman coding, Shannon
coding, Huffman--

01:18:56.140 --> 01:19:00.070
sorry, I repeated that,
Arithmetic coding, ANS.

01:19:00.070 --> 01:19:01.990
But so far, we have--

01:19:01.990 --> 01:19:03.460
we really had a
strong assumption.

01:19:03.460 --> 01:19:06.220
Like we are talking
about entropy,

01:19:06.220 --> 01:19:11.270
but everywhere, we assume
that the symbols were IID.

01:19:11.270 --> 01:19:13.730
But in real life,
you can have symbols

01:19:13.730 --> 01:19:15.470
which are highly correlated.

01:19:15.470 --> 01:19:19.880
The next symbol might not be
independent of the previous one.

01:19:19.880 --> 01:19:23.240
Take anything, take
language, like the symbols

01:19:23.240 --> 01:19:24.920
are highly correlated.

01:19:24.920 --> 01:19:27.872
In the next few lectures will
go into the details of how

01:19:27.872 --> 01:19:29.330
do you actually
work with real data

01:19:29.330 --> 01:19:32.210
which you see, how do you
deal with this correlation

01:19:32.210 --> 01:19:35.450
while ensuring lossless
compression performance,

01:19:35.450 --> 01:19:38.240
and how do you do it
adaptively, how do you like--

01:19:38.240 --> 01:19:43.910
OK, some teaser trailer, how do
you use things like LLMs to get

01:19:43.910 --> 01:19:46.440
your state-of-the-art
compressor.

01:19:46.440 --> 01:19:46.940
Yeah.

01:19:46.940 --> 01:19:49.850
So I think we are going
to change the direction

01:19:49.850 --> 01:19:52.280
a little bit, but whatever
we have been studying is

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:55.030
going to keep building on that.

01:19:55.030 --> 01:19:55.530
Yeah.

01:19:55.530 --> 01:19:57.350
Thank you.

